<h1>How it works</h1>

<p class="lead">Inputs, a section through the room, a result as a tendency. This page explains what is calculated, what is assumed and where the model stops.</p>

<h2>What is calculated</h2>

<p>The app does not draw the air flow by hand. It calculates air flow and temperature. For the room, a flow solver divides the slice into small cells and works out, step by step, air flow and temperature: how warm air rises, cool air sinks and the air moves around a curtain or a sofa. For the wall, it calculates how heat flows through each layer of the wall and how warm the inner surface stays, with room air fixed at 20 °C (common assumption); the wall view is calculated on its own, not from the air picture. From the room air temperature and humidity, it calculates the dew point, and from the inner surface temperature the humidity at the surface.</p>

<p>The model is two-dimensional. While the radiator keeps up, the room air is held at the temperature set for the scene. Its limits are listed below.</p>

<h2>1. Inputs</h2>

<p>In the room view you choose the window, the wall, the radiator setting, the outside temperature, the room size and a curtain or a sofa at fixed positions in front of the window. In the wall view you choose the outdoor temperature, the relative humidity in the room and a wall template (for example an uninsulated wall or one insulated on the outside). The view shows the result for the flat wall and, in one line, for the outside edge.</p>

<h2>2. The 2D section</h2>

<p>The app calculates two things, separately:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Wall surfaces.</strong> The temperature of the wall surface comes from steady-state heat conduction through the wall, calculated as a case with common boundary conditions. At an outside edge the calculation is two-dimensional. A real room corner is usually colder than this edge.</li>
    <li><strong>Air in the room.</strong> The air movement, such as the circulation above the radiator or cool air sinking at the window, is a two-dimensional picture. Real room air is three-dimensional and turbulent. This layer therefore shows mechanisms, not numbers. The colors show warmer and cooler air in broad steps, on a fixed °C scale, as an average over the last minute of model time; the markers show the movement at this moment.</li>
</ul>


<h2>3. The result</h2>

<p>For the wall surface, the app shows a risk estimate in three levels with a range, based on common design values. It shows where the wall gets critically cool in the model.</p>

<h2>The physics inside</h2>

<p><strong>Room air.</strong> The app solves the equations of buoyant air flow (the Boussinesq equations) in two dimensions. The slice is divided into a grid of cells about 3 cm wide: 80 cells from floor to ceiling in a room 2.5 m high, 96 at 3.0 m, 112 at 3.5 m. In every time step the solver works out temperature and velocity in each cell, keeps the amount of air in each cell balanced, and moves on. Curtain and sofa are solid obstacles in this grid: the air has to go around them, or, for the curtain, can pass only a little through the fabric.</p>

<p><strong>Checked against a reference case.</strong> For a textbook case – air in a square box heated on one side and cooled on the other (de Vahl Davis, 1983) – the flow solver, on its finest test grid (128 × 128 cells), matches the published reference values to within 2 %. This tests the solver, not your room: the live view runs on a much coarser grid and in a different flow regime, so there it shows trends only.</p>

<p><strong>Wall.</strong> The wall view is calculated on its own, with room air fixed at 20 °C (common assumption); it does not take the room air from the flow solver. The wall is calculated layer by layer with steady heat conduction, each layer with its own thermal conductivity and a range. The outside edge comes from a two-dimensional heat conduction calculation done in advance for each wall template. From the room air temperature and humidity, the app calculates the dew point. From the inner surface temperature, it calculates the humidity at the surface.</p>

<p><strong>What this means.</strong> Carefully built physics: on its finest test grid, the flow solver is a good numerical approximation of a 2D model. The live view runs on a coarser grid and shows trends, not values for your room; it has not been checked against real rooms.</p>

<h2>Where the values come from</h2>

<p>Every number in the app is labelled:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>calculated</strong> – a result of the simulation from your inputs,</li>
    <li><strong>common assumption</strong> – a widely used design value, for example for heat transfer at surfaces,</li>
    <li><strong>team assumption</strong> – a value we have set as long as no reliable source is available. The app says so.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limits</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Two dimensions instead of three. Corners, roller shutter boxes and junctions in the room are missing.</li>
    <li>Steady state: no daily cycle, no airing with open windows, no heat storage in the wall.</li>
    <li>The wall is a template, not the real build-up of a particular house.</li>
    <li>The air movement is qualitative. The app does not derive statements about draughts in a real room from it.</li>
    <li>Swirls: single swirls come and go. What shows the trend is the overall direction of the air.</li>
    <li>Internal insulation is not covered. It needs a different kind of calculation, with moisture moving through the wall.</li>
    <li>Furniture in front of the wall is not covered in the wall view.</li>
</ul>

<p class="notice">If you can see mould: have the wall looked at on site by a professional.</p>

